<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the test the framework volunteers for</p>
    <h2 id="t-handedness">The Galaxy Handedness Test, With Its Outcomes Written in Advance</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Everything else on this page is a challenge brought against the framework. This
      one it brings against itself, and it is the only place in the work where the sky is given a clean
      opportunity to say no.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <p>The claim under test comes from <a href="/moebius/#t-two-being">the two-being reading of the
        seam</a>: if the half-twist births an inverted pair rather than one oriented thing, there exists a
        plane across which observed galaxy handedness reverses. The prediction is not the size of the
        asymmetry &mdash; the framework has no amplitude and says so &mdash; but its
        <em>shape</em>. A genuine seam demands an orientation field that is cleanly odd about a single
        great circle:</p>
      <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">A(&minus;n&#770;) = &minus;A(n&#770;)</p>
      <p>The rival explanation, and currently the favourite, is pedestrian: galaxies turning against the
        Milky Way's motion are Doppler-brightened, enter a magnitude-limited sample preferentially, and
        produce an excess centred on our Galactic pole that worsens with faintness. That account predicts
        an excess tied to our motion vector and apparent magnitude &mdash; which need not be perfectly
        antisymmetric and need not resolve to one circle. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>The three outcomes, agreed to before the data</h3>
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th>What survives bias-modelling</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A single, clean, perfectly odd dipole</td>
            <td>A real point for the seam, against pure selection.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A dipole, but <em>not</em> aligned with our Galactic pole</td>
            <td>Strongly helps the seam: a cosmic-frame feature cannot be an artefact of our vantage.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The signal dissolves into the Milky-Way frame</td>
            <td>The two beings were a beautiful picture and nothing more. Withdrawn.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">The framework's own expectation runs partly against the current data. A primordial
        seam ought to be a cosmic-frame structure, and the reported axis sits close to our own pole. That
        is a point <em>against</em>, it is recorded here rather than in a footnote, and no attempt is made
        to explain it away.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- expand:stress-tests:end -->

<div class="divider">&#10022;</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the list the framework keeps against itself</p>
    <h2 id="t-new-tests">Twelve More Ways to Break This</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">A stress test is only worth printing if the failure condition is written before
      the result is known. Each of these says what would have to happen for the framework to be wrong,
      and two of them have already been run &mdash; one of them against us.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <h3 id="t-run-already">Two that have already been run, in this repository</h3>

      <h4 id="t-run-base">Experiment 1 &mdash; is 666 a fact about the world or about base ten?</h4>
      <p>The spiral chapter leans on 666 being the thirty-sixth triangular number <em>and</em> a repdigit:
        three identical figures. If that conjunction is common across bases, it is a fact about notation
        and the chapter should stop citing it. So we counted. For every base from 2 to 36 we found every
        triangular number up to 10<sup>12</sup> whose representation is three or more identical non-zero
        digits.</p>
      <p>The result does not flatter us. Fifteen of the thirty-five bases have at least one.
        <strong>Base ten has exactly one &mdash; 666 &mdash; while base nine has eleven</strong>
        (111, 1111, 11111, 111111 and more, all triangular) <strong>and base twenty-five has seven.</strong>
        Base seventeen produces a repdigit triangular with ten identical digits.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="lede">On this measure base ten is not rich. It is one of the sparser bases that manage the
        trick at all, and 666 is its only instance. The honest conclusion is that the repdigit property of
        666 carries no weight, and the spiral chapter should rest on 666 = T<sub>36</sub> = 36&middot;37/2
        and the quark count alone &mdash; which is base-independent and survives intact.</p>

      <h4 id="t-run-pisano">Experiment 2 &mdash; is the Pisano period of ten distinguished?</h4>
      <p>Here the answer goes the other way, modestly. The Fibonacci sequence modulo n repeats with period
        &pi;(n), and &pi;(10) = 60. Computing &pi;(n) for every n up to 40, the ratio &pi;(n)/n is
        maximised at n = 10, where it reaches exactly 6 &mdash; higher than every other base in the range.
        That is not a coincidence of our search: it is a known theorem that &pi;(n) &le; 6n with equality
        exactly when n = 2&middot;5<sup>k</sup>, and we confirmed it numerically at n = 10, 50 and 250.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="muted">So base ten attains the maximum possible Fibonacci period for its size, and it is the
        smallest base that does. That is a real distinction and it is a narrow one: the property belongs to
        the family 10, 50, 250, and the framework may claim only that ten is the first member, not that ten
        is unique.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

