          <text x="86" y="66" fill="#ffd979">λ &gt; 0: chaotic &mdash; prediction decays exponentially</text>
          <text x="86" y="84" fill="#ef84b6">undamped: never negative, anywhere</text>
          <text x="86" y="102" fill="#cdefff">damped: dips below zero again and again</text>
          <text x="86" y="318" fill="#45d6b4">λ &lt; 0: periodic &mdash; the motion settles into a cycle</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">Two sweeps. The pale curve is the damped pendulum; the rose curve is the same
      system with the damping removed. Each point is a separate integration of the driven damped pendulum at
      q&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.5 and &omega;&nbsp;=&nbsp;2/3, run for three thousand time units after a discarded
      transient, with the tangent vector renormalised every unit. Where a curve sits above the gold line
      the motion is genuinely chaotic. The damped curve dips below repeatedly &mdash; the same equation,
      perfectly periodic. The undamped curve never does, at any amplitude tested.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </figure>

</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h3 id="t-lyap-windows">What the sweep found, which the framework did not want</h3>
    <p>Computing λ at one parameter set proves one parameter set. So the exponent was swept across drive
      amplitudes from 0.00 to 1.60 in steps of 0.025, and the result is the plate above.</p>
    <p class="lede"><strong>Chaos is not generic.</strong> It occupies two narrow bands &mdash; roughly
      A = 1.15 to 1.27, and again near A = 1.50 &mdash; with the
      exponent peaking at λ = +0.1583 at A = 1.18. Between and beyond those bands the same
      equation is periodic, sometimes sharply so: at A = 1.30 and A = 1.35 the exponent returns to the
      fully contracted value of &minus;0.25. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>This is a new debt and it was created by the framework's own experiment, which is the point of
      running them. The thesis says the universe swings like a pendulum that cannot settle, and the stronger reading of
      that sentence used to be that the swing is chaotic. The computation
      says a driven pendulum is out of control only inside particular windows of its parameters, and is
      otherwise as regular as a metronome. <strong>So the framework now owes an account of why its
      parameters would sit inside a chaotic band rather than in one of the much wider periodic
      regions</strong> &mdash; and it does not have one. Saying the cosmos is chaotic because pendulums
      are chaotic is no longer available: most pendulums, most of the time, are not.</p>
    <p class="muted">The repair, if there is one, is that a cosmic system is undamped, and an undamped
      system cannot settle into the attracting cycles that produce the negative exponents here. That was
      written as plausible and uncomputed. It is computed in the next section.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <h3 id="t-lyap-undamped">Experiment four &mdash; the undamped case, which the last section demanded</h3>
    <p>The repair offered above was that a cosmic system has no outside to lose energy to, so it cannot
      settle into the attracting cycles that produce the negative exponents &mdash; and that this was
      plausible but uncomputed. It has now been computed: the same sweep with the damping set to zero,
      amplitudes from 0 to 1.6.</p>
    <p class="lede">The result is unambiguous. <strong>The undamped exponent is never negative at any
      amplitude tested</strong> &mdash; the minimum over the whole sweep is
      +0.00001, which is zero to within the integration error. The periodic windows are
      <em>gone</em>. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>And this is not a numerical accident; it could not have come out otherwise. An undamped system
      preserves phase-space area, so by Liouville's theorem its exponents must sum to zero, which in two
      dimensions forces λ&#8321; = &minus;λ&#8322; and therefore λ&#8321; &ge; 0. <strong>A negative
      largest exponent is mathematically impossible without dissipation.</strong> The computation agrees
      with the theorem, which is the correct order of events. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Above A &asymp; 0.40 the exponent sits around +0.16 across the entire range,
      with no windows and no returns to regularity &mdash; chaos is <em>generic</em> once the drive is
      strong enough. Below that the exponent is zero rather than negative: the motion is quasi-periodic,
      wandering on an intact torus, still never settling but perfectly predictable.</p>
    <p class="lede">So the debt the sweep created is paid, and the two halves of the thesis sentence come
      apart cleanly in the process. <strong>Cannot settle</strong> is now the stronger claim: it holds at
      every amplitude, and it holds by theorem rather than by computation.
      <strong>Out of control</strong> is the weaker one: it holds above a threshold and fails below it,
      where the motion is unending and entirely foreseeable.</p>
    <p class="muted">Stated against ourselves, because the repair should not be allowed to buy more than
      it paid for: all of this is a two-dimensional driven pendulum, and Liouville's theorem applies to
      the universe only if the universe is Hamiltonian, which is exactly the sort of thing a framework
      should not help itself to. What has been shown is that <em>the framework's own chosen system</em>
      behaves as the framework said it would once the dissipation is removed. That was in doubt an hour
      ago and it is not in doubt now.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- lyapunov:end -->

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h3 id="t-proposed">Ten proposed, with kill conditions attached</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap scroll-x">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Test</th><th>What it probes</th><th>What kills the framework</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Matched circles in the sky</strong></td>
            <td>A twisted or non-trivially connected cosmos should put the same circle of sky in two
              places. The Cornish&ndash;Spergel&ndash;Starkman search is the standard method.</td>
            <td>Searches have come back empty. The megatubule must say at what scale it evades them, or
              concede that the throat is unobservable in principle &mdash; which costs it the Cold Spot
              as evidence.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Cosmic birefringence</strong></td>
            <td>A handed universe should rotate the plane of CMB polarisation. A non-zero angle of about
              0.3 degrees has been reported and is not yet confirmed.</td>
            <td>The Möbius reading must pre-register a <em>sign</em>. If LiteBIRD measures the angle and
              the sign is wrong, the chapter is finished.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Drift in the constants</strong></td>
            <td>If constants are running totals of residue, they should accrue.</td>
            <td>Atomic clocks already hold the fine-structure drift below one part in 10<sup>18</sup> per
              year and Oklo holds it flat for two billion years. The ledger must predict a drift smaller
              than that and say how much smaller, or admit the reading is unfalsifiable.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>The radial acceleration relation</strong></td>
            <td>χ proportional to 1/r gives flat rotation curves. Real galaxies obey a tighter law than
              that.</td>
            <td>Fit the SPARC sample. If the χ-field cannot reproduce the observed scatter and the
              Tully&ndash;Fisher slope of four, flat curves were never the hard part and the model fails
              where it matters.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Proton decay</strong></td>
            <td>One thing at two phases implies a conversion.</td>
            <td>Hyper-Kamiokande reaching about 7.8 × 10<sup>34</sup> years with nothing seen weakens this
              and every grand unified theory alike.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Flicker fusion against metabolic rate</strong></td>
            <td>If the grain is issued by thermodynamics, the length of a perceptual moment should scale
              with metabolism across species. Critical flicker-fusion data already exist.</td>
            <td>No scaling, or scaling with body size rather than metabolic rate, and the Landauer
              argument loses its one biological handle.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Reconsolidation, quantified</strong></td>
            <td>The claim is that awareness is paid for out of memory. That is a rate, not a mood.</td>
            <td>Degradation on recall that does not scale with the depth of attention paid. The qualitative
              effect is established; the proportionality is the framework's and is unmeasured.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>A toy integrator</strong></td>
            <td>Build the smallest simulation with two opposed infinities and see whether an arrow appears
              without one being inserted by hand.</td>
            <td>If no arrow emerges, or one emerges only because the code put it there, the entropy
              objection is answered in the negative by the framework's own machinery.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>An actual Lyapunov exponent</strong></td>
            <td>The thesis calls the swing chaotic. Chaos is a defined property with a number attached.</td>
            <td>No computable exponent means the word must be dropped. Borrowing the glamour of chaos
              without the measurement is exactly the failure this page exists to catch.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pre-registered galaxy spins in Euclid</strong></td>
            <td>The handedness claim, done properly: method, sample and sign lodged before the data.</td>
            <td>Any result at all, provided the prediction was filed first. An unregistered reanalysis
              after the fact is worth nothing and the framework will not count it.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">Two of these the framework can run itself with no telescope and no funding &mdash; the
      toy integrator and the Lyapunov exponent &mdash; which makes them the next pieces of work rather
      than hopes. The other eight depend on instruments, and four of them on instruments already
      collecting.</p>
  </div>
</section>

